[2026-06-12 19:11:10] [claude-sdk] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0118BJaJdmNWEzWTbJxf8U2r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the data flow:\n\n1. Backend controller wraps: `{ success: true, data: assetSummary }`\n2. The service returns the full GraphQL response, so `assetSummary` = `{ getAssetSummary: { cpu: {...}, memory: {...}, disk: {...} } }`\n3. So the full response from the backend is: `{ success: true, data: { getAssetSummary: { cpu: {...}, memory: {...}, disk: {...} } } }`\n\nNow in the mobile app:\n1. `getAssetSummary(assetId)` does `res.data` (axios unwrap) — so we get `{ success: true, data: { getAssetSummary: { ... } } }`\n2. In the component, I'm extracting the nested data by first checking if the promise fulfilled, then unwrapping the response structure to get the actual asset summary object with cpu, memory, and disk metrics.\n\n3. From there I'm destructuring those metrics with fallbacks to empty objects, then I need to verify what format these values are in — whether `cpuUsage`, `memoryUsage`, and `driveUsage` are objects with `{ value, unit }` properties or raw numbers based on the GraphQL schema.\n\nLooking at the `metricValue` helper function, it handles both cases: if the input is an object with a `value` property, it extracts that number; if it's already a number, it returns it directly. So the data flow should work correctly whether the API returns structured objects or raw values.\n\nNow I'm wondering if the data is actually coming through correctly but something about how it's being displayed or rendered is broken. It could be a speech-to-text issue where \"now\" was meant to be \"not\" — so the CPU, memory, and disk data isn't displaying at all. The most likely culprit is that the summary API call is failing or returning null values, which would cause all the bars to show dashes. Or they might be referring to the Device section that displays CPU name, architecture, and RAM information rather than the utilization bars themselves. The device might not have summary data available, or the API could be returning an unexpected format. Let me trace through my rewrite more carefully—I moved the asset variable definition before the submitTicket function, but that shouldn't matter since the function only executes on button press. I'm comparing my changes against the original code to see if I accidentally introduced a bug. The closure captures the variable `a` by reference, so even though it's defined after `submitTicket`, the function won't execute until the button is clicked, by which point `a` will already be initialized. Moving the declaration before `submitTicket` is actually the cleaner approach anyway.\n\nNow I'm looking at the rendering issue with the `LinearGradient` component in `UtilBar`. The gradient is being rendered inside a flex container with a percentage-based width, which might not work properly in React Native — the gradient likely needs explicit pixel dimensions or flex properties to display correctly. The Device Details section displays CPU name, architecture, RAM, and disk size, but they all show dashes when the summary data hasn't loaded. Since the user mentioned this data is \"now coming through,\" it sounds like the API call for summary information might be failing or delayed — possibly a CORS issue or network problem that wasn't happening before. Let me think about what could've changed to break that data flow.\n\nI'm reviewing the data fetching logic I rewrote for the asset detail page, using Promise.allSettled to handle multiple API calls at once. I'm checking how the summary response is being processed and whether the data extraction is working correctly. The data fetching logic stayed the same, so the issue must be with how the LinearGradient bars are rendering or possibly a backend data problem. Let me reconsider what the user actually meant—they said the CPU, memory, and disk information \"is now coming through,\" which might mean it's actually working and they're asking me to verify or fix something else entirely. I'm testing different scenarios to understand what's happening — whether the data structure is an object with value and unit properties or just a plain number, both should work with the current function. Since I can't pinpoint the issue theoretically, I should try a more practical approach: simplify the gradient rendering logic to avoid potential issues, and add better error handling to surface what's actually going wrong. The problem might also be with how the `expo-linear-gradient` module is being loaded or rendered. I'm leaning toward replacing the LinearGradient with a simple View and backgroundColor since that's more reliable on devices, but I'm second-guessing the user's phrasing—\"is now coming through\" with \"Can you fix that?\" suggests they actually mean the data *isn't* displaying. If it's a speech-to-text issue, they're probably saying the CPU, memory, and disk information isn't showing up at all, which could be either the UtilBar gradient bars failing to render or the data values themselves not appearing in the Device section.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":988,"cache_read_input_tokens":101864,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":988},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab312fcf-52d9-4bfd-b9f5-2b71e9f3b92d","uuid":"3fe972e1-ded5-4672-9232-4415f1e10553"}
