[2026-06-14 17:04:24] [claude-sdk] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X5qrp1FRMZq7yG3wwGPcDq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, the user said \"move the logo up another 20%\". The current marginTop is spacing.xxxl which is 40. 20% more up would mean increasing the marginTop further... but actually \"move up\" on screen means the logo should appear higher, which could mean either increasing the top margin to push it away from the safe area downward (which moves it down, not up) or reducing padding above it.\n\nActually, I think \"move up\" means the user wants the logo to appear higher on the screen. The marginTop on brandWrap pushes it DOWN from the top. So to move it UP, I should REDUCE the marginTop, not increase it.\nBut wait, the user kept saying \"up\" and seemed satisfied when I was increasing marginTop, so maybe they're thinking of it differently. The heroContent has paddingBottom and the brandWrap is the first child. Since the SafeAreaView handles the top and the hero uses `justifyContent: 'flex-end'`, adding more marginTop actually pushes the logo down within that flex container. Yet the user's feedback suggested they wanted the direction I was going, so maybe they mean they want the logo positioned higher in the overall hero section.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the issue: with `justifyContent: 'flex-end'` pushing content to the bottom, marginTop on brandWrap won't move it up visually. To actually move the logo higher on screen, I'd need to either increase the hero's minHeight to create more space, switch away from flex-end alignment, or add more paddingBottom to heroContent to shift everything upward. I think the simplest approach is to just increase the paddingBottom on heroContent to push the entire content block upward, since the parent container uses flex-end alignment. Let me adjust that value instead of tweaking the logo's marginTop. Right, so the marginTop on brandWrap actually does push the logo up because flex-end anchors the content to the bottom, making the content block taller extends it upward. The user wants 20% more than the current 48, so I'll bump it to 58.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":254,"cache_read_input_tokens":88691,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":254},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab312fcf-52d9-4bfd-b9f5-2b71e9f3b92d","uuid":"c811afd1-4ef0-4f07-a5f3-7e96ca70d106"}
